<SiteLayout title="A Practical CRM Follow-Up Process for New Leads" description="A practical framework for routing new inquiries, responding quickly, tracking progress and avoiding missed sales opportunities." path="/insights/crm-follow-up-for-new-leads/">
<article><header class="lf-article-hero"><div class="container"><p class="section-kicker"><a href="/insights/">INSIGHTS</a> / LEAD GENERATION</p><h1>A Practical CRM Follow-Up Process for New Leads</h1><p class="lf-lede">Generating a lead is not the finish line. The next steps often decide whether that inquiry becomes a real conversation.</p></div></header><div class="container lf-article-body"><section class="lf-article-section"><h2>Capture reliable contact context</h2><p>Store the source, inquiry type, relevant permissions and any qualification answers that help the team respond appropriately.</p></section>
<section class="lf-article-section"><h2>Assign ownership</h2><p>Decide who handles each lead and how exceptions are surfaced when no owner is available.</p></section>
<section class="lf-article-section"><h2>Respond with context</h2><p>Use messages that acknowledge the actual inquiry. Avoid aggressive, irrelevant or non-consensual sequences.</p></section>
<section class="lf-article-section"><h2>Track meaningful statuses</h2><p>Use clear stages such as new, contacted, qualified, scheduled and closed, with definitions your team understands.</p></section><div class="lf-article-summary"><h2>What to do next</h2><p>Start with a simple pipeline, reliable assignment and timely responses before investing in complicated automation.</p></div><a class="lf-back-link" href="/insights/">← Back to all insights</a></div></article>
</SiteLayout>